{%- comment -%}
  Renders the country picker for the localization form

  Accepts:
    - localPosition: pass in the position in which the form is coming up to create specific IDs
    - abbreviate_mobile: when true, the closed button shows just the
      country code (e.g. "PT") below 750px, and the full "Country |
      CUR $" label at 750px and up. The currency only shows in the
      closed button at the full-label width; it's still visible per
      country in the open dropdown list either way. Mirrors the same
      param on language-localization.liquid, and reuses its CSS
      (.localization-selector--abbreviate-mobile / __label-full /
      __label-abbrev), so both selectors stay visually consistent.
    - merge_locale: when true, each country link also carries a
      data-locale (Portugal -> pt-PT, every other country -> en, since
      there's no per-country translation yet). localization-form.js
      picks that up and follows the country switch with a second,
      automatic locale switch, so this one control replaces a separate
      language picker. Only opt in where there's no language-
      localization snippet alongside it, so the other callers (header,
      footer, mobile drawer) keep their independent language selector
      unaffected.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign currencies = localization.available_countries | map: 'currency' | map: 'iso_code' | uniq
  assign popular_countries = localization.available_countries | where: 'popular?' | sort: 'name'

  assign show_country_filter = false
  if localization.available_countries.size > 9
    assign show_country_filter = true
  endif

  assign show_popular_countries = false
  if localization.available_countries.size > 9 and popular_countries.size > 1
    assign show_popular_countries = true
  endif

  assign show_currencies = false
  if currencies.size > 1
    assign show_currencies = true
  endif
%}

<div class="disclosure">
